You might want to try again with better network conditions (a crossover
cable between two machines being the best setup to prevent other traffic
from interfering with the fragile netboot loader).

> Is the 3000-300x not supported in this snapshot? I noticed that there 
> are no boot floppies that support this machine, and the support is 
> listed as being added post 3.1

I have been able to install on 3000/[35]00 series by burning a bootable
cd-rom with the bsd.rd in the root of the cdrom filesystem, since their
proms are not able to look for subdirectories. And the kernel filename
needs to be uppercase too.

Eventually I plan to have more awareness of these constraints in the
OpenBSD/alpha bootloader, so that these machines boot off the 3.2 (and
later) cdrom sets. But oh well, given how many people are still using
this kind of machines, it's not that high on my todo list.

> I can't even build a kernel on my other alpha since it is a Jensen and 
> is not supported at all (though if testing is needed for that... =)
I might remember this next time I need to lose more sanity.

Miod

From openbsd-alpha  Mon Jul 15 20:55:24 2002
From: Miod Vallat <miod () online ! fr>
Date: Mon, 15 Jul 2002 20:55:24 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Re: DECchip 21066 w/o  SRM console
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=102676632932302

> Today I assembled the Aplha into an old tower chassis and it booted successfully.
> Well, I got my OpenBSD CD and wanted to install... but there was no SRM console, because the 
> machine has the 21066 chipset :-/

I don't see your point.
The periodic tables of alphas only lists the XL series and the 164UX as
lacking SRM.

The fact that your machine uses a 21066 CPU has absolutely nothing to do
with the existence or not of a SRM for it.

It is very likely that an SRM firmware does exist for your alpha, it's
just a matter of fetching it and installing it from the ARCbios.

Of course, then, your system might not be supported by OpenBSD for other
reasons - who knows, you did not tell us which kind of machine you have.

Miod

